  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}-y={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(-1,-2)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(1,-2)$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{\u2032}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{\u2032}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}-y={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\,\u27f9\\,{y}^{\u2032}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{\u2032}|}_{(-1,-2)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(-1,-2)} \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =0\\,\u2713 \\\\ {{y}^{\u2032}|}_{(1,1)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}+(1) \\\\  & =5 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{\u2032}|}_{(1,-2)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+y)|}_{(1,-2)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}+(-2) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
